{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he saw rest.</b> He praised rest, like 'look not at wine when it is red.' For good is Issachar — righteous and discerning, knowing to distinguish the benefit of rest, and not like Zebulun who is in distress when he goes about in merchandise in ships. And so it says: 'Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out,' etc." "<b>And he bent his shoulder to bear.</b> The yoke of the kings of Israel, and in what matter <b>did he become a servant for tribute</b> — to give the kings of Israel a tithe of his produce, as it is written in the book of Samuel: 'and your fields he shall tithe.' Another interpretation: 'and he bent his shoulder to bear' — the burden of his brothers who lodge with him, for whoever needs to pass from his tribe's portion to his brother's portion passes through his land, and he bears the trouble of all of them. That is 'and he became a servant for tribute.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:13.459709127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}